      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the previously derived results for $\\mathbf{M}$.\n\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M}=\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z\\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix}= \\begin{pmatrix} 2x^2+2y^2 & xz-yz & 0 \\\\ xz-yz & 2yz+2z^2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & x^2+y^2+z^2 \\end{pmatrix}\\]\n\n\nThe equation $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}=\\mathbf{I}$ means that\n\\[2x^2+2y^2=1,2yz+2z^2=1,\\]and $x^2+y^2+z^2=1.$\n\nFrom $2x^2+2y^2=1,$ we get $x^2+y^2=\\frac{1}{2}.$  From $2z^2+2yz=1,$ we get $y^2z^2=\\frac{1}{2}.$  Also, $x^2+y^2+z^2=1.$\n\nTherefore, \\[x^2+y^2+z^2=\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\]. The answer is \\frac{1}{2}",
