      "text": "\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M} &= \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + x^2 & 2yx - xy & xz - xz \\\\ 2yx - xy & 4y^2 - y^2 & 2yz - yz \\\\ zx - xz & 2zy + yz & z^2 + z^2 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} 2x^2 & xy & 0 \\\\ xy & 3y^2 & yz \\\\ 0 & yz & 2z^2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}Since matrix $\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M}$ is equal to\n\\[\\mathbf{I} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix},\\] we have the following:\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 &\\equiv 1 \\mod 2, \\\\ xy &\\equiv 0 \\mod 1, \\\\ yz &\\equiv 0 \\mod 1, \\\\ 2z^2 &\\equiv 1 \\mod 2.\n\\end{align*}Because $z$ is even, we also have $z^2\\equiv 0\\mod 2.$  Since $y$ is odd, we must have $y\\equiv 0 \\mod 2.$  Since $x^2\\equiv 1\\mod 2,$ we also have $x$ is odd.  We deduce that $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \\equiv 1 \\mod 2.$  Since $2z^2 \\not\\equiv 0\\mod 2$, we also have $z^2+1\\not\\equiv 0\\mod 2.$  Thus $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \\equiv \\boxed{1}\\mod 2.$The answer is: 1",
